<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chandra Shumsher Jang Bahadur Rana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a>–<a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Chandra Shumsher Rana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chandra Shumsher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jang-bahadur-rana">Jang Bahadur Rana</a> (1863–1929) was the hereditary Rana prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> from 1901 to 1929. He built the Singha Durbar palace, founded Tri-Chandra College, supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and obtained the treaty of 1923 in which Britain formally recognised Nepal's independence. In 1924 he abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in Nepal, freeing tens of thousands of people.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chandra-shumsher-jang-bahadur-rana.jpg" alt="Chandra Shumsher Jang Bahadur Rana" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maharaja_Chandra_Shamsher.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
